#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
